o only maybe \"All Types\"? My hand contains all three suits and honors? With J1 pair, I have honors, and all three suits in chows, so \"All Types\" (6 points). Plus maybe \"All Chows\"? But \"All Chows\" requires the pair to be suited tiles, so with J1 pair, I cannot get \"All Chows\". So patterns: All Types (6) + possibly something else? \"Mixed Straight\"? Not applicable. So 6 points, still below 8. Therefore, neither discard gives me 8 points immediately. I need to improve my hand by drawing tiles that form pungs or kongs, or change melds. But I have four exposed chows, so I cannot change them. So my hand is fixed. The only way to increase points is through the pair and possibly wait patterns like \"Self Drawn\" (1), \"Single Wait\" (1), etc. But those are small. Perhaps \"Last Tile\" patterns? Not sure. Given the situation, I might not be able to win with 8 points unless I get a special draw. But I have to choose a discard now. Between T7 and J1, discarding J1 might allow me to go for \"All Chows\" and \"No Honors\", but only 3 points. Discarding T7 might allow \"All Types\" (6) but still short. However, \"All Types\" is 6 points, and if I add \"Self Drawn\" (1) and \"Single Wait\" (1), that's 8 points? But \"Single Wait\" is only counted if the winning tile is the only tile to complete the pair, which it would be if I am waiting for J1. And \"Self Drawn\" if I draw it myself. So potential total: All Types (6) + Single Wait (1) + Self Drawn (1) = 8 points. That meets the threshold. For T7 pair, I could have All Chows (2) + No Honors (1) + Single Wait (1) + Self Drawn (1) = 5 points, not enough. Therefore, keeping J1 and discarding T7 gives a better chance to reach 8 points if I can get Single Wait and Self Drawn. Also, J1 has 3 copies left, T7 has 2 left, so J1 is slightly more available. Moreover, if I draw another J1, I could have a pung of J1, giving Dragon Pung (2 points), and then I'd need a pair from somewhere else, but I have no other tiles, so I'd need to change a chow into a pair? Not possible with exposed chows. So pung of J1 would require me to have four tiles? Actually, if I get a pung of J1, that would be three J1s, and I still need a pair. But I have only one tile left after forming pung, so I'd need to draw a tile to pair with that leftover? Complicated. So probably sticking with pair is simpler. Therefore, I should discard T7 to keep J1 and aim for a J1 pair with Single Wait and Self Drawn to reach 8 points.\n\nReason: Discarding T7 keeps J1, which allows for the \"All Types\" pattern (6 points) since my hand contains all three suits and an honor tile. Combined with \"Single Wait\" (1 point) if waiting for J1 as the only winning tile, and \"Self Drawn\" (1 point) if I draw it myself, I can reach the required 8 points to win. Discarding J1 would only yield lower-scoring patterns like \"All Chows\" and \"No Honors\" totaling 3 points, with less potential to reach 8 even with additional wait patterns. Additionally, J1 has more unseen copies (3) than T7 (2), increasing the chance of drawing it.\n\nAnswer: Play T7"}
