keep J1 \u2192 if I draw J1, I win with: All Chows (2), Dragon Pung (2, if it's a pung? No, it's a pair, not a pung \u2014 Dragon Pung requires a pung or kong, so a pair of dragons doesn't count), plus maybe other patterns. Wait, I\u2019d have four chows and a pair of honors \u2192 that breaks All Simples, but I could have Pung of Terminals or Honors (1 point) if the pair is considered? No, \"Pung of Terminals or Honors\" requires a pung/kong, not a pair. So I\u2019d only have All Chows (2) + maybe Self-Drawn (1) if I draw it myself. That's only 3 points, not enough to win (need 8).  \n\nOption 2: Discard J1, keep B6 \u2192 if I draw B6, I win with: All Chows (2) + All Simples (2) = 4 points, still not enough.  \n\nI need at least 8 points. Currently, my hand\u2019s melds are all chows, all simples (if I discard J1), but that\u2019s only 4 points. I need more patterns.  \n\nPossible patterns if I change wait:  \n- If I keep J1 and later draw another J1 to make a pair, I could try to change a meld to get more points? But I can\u2019t change melds\u2014they\u2019re fixed.  \n- Maybe wait for a tile that gives me \"Mixed Double Chow\" or \"Pure Double Chow\"? But my melds are already set.  \n- Maybe \"Two Terminal Chows\"? I have no terminal chows (W7-W9 is terminal? W9 is terminal, so W7-W9 chow includes terminal 9, so that\u2019s one terminal chow. T2-T4 is not terminal. So I have one terminal chow (W7-W9). If I had another, I could get Two Terminal Chows (1 point), but I don\u2019t.  \n- \"Edge Wait\" or \"Closed Wait\"? Possibly if my winning tile is edge/closed wait, but that\u2019s only 1 point.  \n\nGiven the low scoring potential, I might need to consider robbing a kong or last tile, but that\u2019s uncertain.  \n\nBut looking at history: many tiles are out. B6: I have one in hand, one in my meld [B4,B5,B6], so two are used. Unseen B6: 1 left. J1: I have one in hand, unseen: 3 left. Others have melded F1, F4, etc.  \n\nGiven the need for points, maybe keep J1 because if I draw a third J1, I can declare a kong (if possible) and get Concealed Kong (2) + Dragon Pung (2) + maybe more. But I only have one J1 now, so drawing two more is unlikely.  \n\nActually, better to think: My hand is almost ready\u2014I just need a pair. The quickest win is to keep the tile with more unseen. So keep J1, discard B6.  \n\nBut points: Even if I win with J1 pair, I likely won\u2019t reach 8. Maybe I should not win yet and try to improve hand? But in MCR, you can only win if hand is 8+ points. I must aim for a wait that yields enough points. Currently, with B6 pair, I get All Chows + All Simples = 4, plus maybe Self-Drawn = 1 \u2192 5, still short. With J1 pair, even less (no All Simples). So neither wait gives 8.  \n\nI need to change my hand\u2014but I can\u2019t change melds. The only way is to get a new meld by picking up a tile and breaking a chow? But that\u2019s not allowed in valid actions\u2014only play.  \n\nWait\u2014valid actions are only Play B6 or Play J1. So I must discard one. After discard, I draw a tile next turn. Maybe I can draw something that lets me reorganize? But with only two tiles in hand, after draw I\u2019ll have three, still need to discard one.  \n\nGiven the situation, I should probably discard J1 to keep All Simples possibility, and hope to draw a tile that can be paired and also gives more points via other patterns (like a dragon or wind to make a pung, but that\u2019s unlikely with only two tiles left).  \n\nAlternatively, discard B6 to keep J1, and hope to draw another J1 to make a pung later, giving Dragon Pung (2) + maybe Two Concealed Pungs (2) if I get another concealed pung\u2014but I have no concealed pungs, all melds are exposed chows.  \n\nActually, all my melds are exposed (Chi actions), so no concealed pungs possible. So Concealed Kong not possible unless I get a concealed kong from scratch now.  \n\nGiven the low chance of reaching 8 points with current hand, maybe the best is to discard the less useful tile for defense? But no obvious dangerous tile.  \n\nGiven scoring, All Simples (2) + All Chows (2) + maybe Self-Drawn (1) + maybe Last Tile (4) if it\u2019s last tile? But we don\u2019t know if it\u2019s last tile.  \n\nI think the safest is to discard J1 to maintain All Simples chance, and hope for extra points from wait patterns or last tile.  \n\nReason: Keeping B6 maintains All Simples (2 points) and All Chows (2 points), giving a base of 4 points. If I win by self-drawing B6, that adds Self-Drawn (1) = 5. If the B6 is the last available B6 (three others are used? Let\u2019s check: my meld has one B6, my hand has one, so two B6s used. Unseen: 1. So if I draw that last B6, it could qualify as Last Tile (4 points) if it\u2019s the last tile of its kind? \"Last Tile\" pattern requires the winning tile to be the last tile of the same tile not revealed \u2014 meaning three of same tile exposed in melds or discarded. B6: one in my meld (exposed), one in my hand (not exposed), plus maybe others discarded? History shows B6 discarded by previous player (Play B6), and by me (Play B6 in history? Actually in my history: \"Play B6\" appears once, so that\u2019s a discard). So at least one B6 discarded. That means three B6s known? My meld B6 is one, my discard is one, previous player\u2019s discard is one \u2014 that\u2019s three. So if I draw the last B6, it would be the fourth B6, so not \"Last Tile\" pattern (which requires three of the tile already known). So no Last Tile points.  \n\nThus, B6 pair gives at most 5 points, not enough.  \n\nJ1 pair: J1 unseen: 3. If I draw a J1, it\u2019s not last tile. Points: All Chows (2) + maybe Pung of Terminals or Honors? No, because pair not pung. So only 2 points, far from 8.  \n\nTherefore, with current hand, I cannot win with 8 points. I must change hand by drawing new tiles and possibly breaking a meld? But that\u2019s long-term.  \n\nGiven that, I should discard the tile that might be safer. J1 is a dragon, possibly dangerous if someone needs it for big dragon hand? But across player has F4 pung, previous has F1 pung, no dragon pungs shown. Next player\u2019s history shows Play J2, Play J3, so maybe they have dragons? Unclear.  \n\nGiven the low risk, I\u2019ll discard J1 to keep hand more flexible for All Simples.  \n\nBut actually, I just need to choose one. I\u2019ll choose Play J1.  \n\nWait, but if I discard J1, I keep B6.  \n\nI think discard J1 is better.  \n\n**Reason:** My current hand cannot reach 8 points with either B6 or J1 as the pair. However, discarding J1 keeps the hand eligible for All Simples (2 points) along with All Chows (2 points), providing a higher base score (4 points) compared to keeping J1 (which loses All Simples). Although still short of 8, it leaves more potential to draw a tile that can be paired and also add other point patterns (e.g., if I draw a tile that gives Edge Wait or Closed Wait). Additionally, J1 is a dragon tile with 3 remaining, which could be riskier to hold if opponents are collecting dragons, but no strong indication of that. Thus, discarding J1 is the slightly better tactical move.\n\n**Answer:** Play J1"}
